# Pi4B PORT - append to /boot/firmware/config.txt (Bookworm moved the boot
# partition from /boot to /boot/firmware; older Raspberry Pi OS releases used
# /boot directly - check which one you're on before editing).
#
# Background: Pi4B's USB-C port is wired to the SoC's internal dwc2
# controller's data lines (unlike some early rumors/forum confusion - this was
# confirmed on real Pi4B hardware: forums.raspberrypi.com/viewtopic.php?t=391191,
# blog.hardill.me.uk/2019/11/02/pi4-usb-c-gadget). This is the *same* dwc2 core
# used by Pi Zero W's micro-USB port, so P4wnP1's configfs/libcomposite gadget
# code (service/SubSysUSB.go) needs zero changes - it only ever talks to
# /sys/kernel/config/usb_gadget and /sys/class/udc, both generic Linux gadget
# framework paths, not anything Pi0-specific.
#
# dr_mode=peripheral forces the controller into device/gadget mode
# deterministically at boot, rather than relying on OTG ID-pin auto-sensing
# (which is what you'd want if you also wanted this port to act as a USB host
# sometimes - P4wnP1 never wants that, it should always come up as a gadget).
#
# Note: the four USB-A ports (via the VL805 xHCI controller) are completely
# separate hardware from the dwc2 core and are NOT affected by this - they
# stay in host mode always. This is actually new capability vs Pi Zero W: you
# can plug in a USB wifi adapter, external storage, etc. into the USB-A ports
# *while* the USB-C port is simultaneously acting as the HID/RNDIS/mass-storage
# gadget. Pi Zero W had no spare host-mode USB port at all.
dtoverlay=dwc2,dr_mode=peripheral

# Not required for gadget mode itself, but if you ever want to fall back to
# USB-C host mode for development/debugging (e.g. plugging in a keyboard
# directly), the community-documented flip is:
#   sudo modprobe -r dwc2
#   sudo dtoverlay dwc2 dr_mode=host
#   sudo modprobe dwc2
# (forums.raspberrypi.com/viewtopic.php?t=246348)
